[% set TECHNOLOGY_STANDARDS -%]
### Python

- Use Python 3.12 or newer.
- Keep importable code under the `src` layout.
- Add parameter and return annotations to production functions and public APIs.
- Require Google-style docstrings for public APIs; do not repeat annotations or
  document trivial private implementation details.
- Keep strict static typing enabled for production code and tests.
- Use `Any` only at genuinely untyped boundaries and narrow it immediately.
- Keep casts and type suppressions narrow and explain why they are necessary.
- Include the exact diagnostic code in every type or lint suppression.
- Do not globally ignore missing imports or disable strict checking.
- Use explicit imports. Do not use wildcard imports.
- Keep module import behavior free of network, filesystem and process side
  effects.
- Do not use mutable default arguments.
- Do not use `assert` for external validation or behavior required in optimized
  execution.
- Use timezone-aware UTC datetimes.
- Use `pathlib.Path` for filesystem paths.
- Specify text encoding explicitly for persisted or exchanged text.
- Use context managers for files, connections, locks and other resources.
- Do not perform blocking I/O inside asynchronous functions.
- Introduce asynchronous code only when concurrent I/O or an external framework
  requires it.
- Catch explicit exception types.
- Never catch `BaseException`.
- Catch broad `Exception` only at a process, task or protocol boundary where it
  is logged and translated consistently.
- Preserve causes when translating exceptions.
- Keep package initialization lightweight and free of runtime orchestration.
- Expose public package APIs intentionally rather than through incidental
  imports.

Framework rules are capability-owned. Pydantic, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy,
migrations, and framework-specific asynchronous rules are added only when the
selected project architecture requires them.

[%- endset %]

[% set COVERAGE_REQUIREMENTS -%]
- Measure only production code under `src/[[ package_name ]]`.
- Enable Coverage.py branch measurement.
- Require overall combined statement-and-branch coverage of at least
  [[ coverage_target ]]%.
- The generated default is 95%.
- The scaffold must not recommend or generate a target below 90%.
- Offer a higher target when the project has a small, deterministic or
  safety-critical codebase.
- Require 100% only for explicitly identified critical modules where the
  boundary is meaningful and enforceable with Coverage.py.
- Do not include tests, generated files or vendored code in the measured source.
- Keep every exclusion narrow and explained.
- Do not introduce a custom parser to calculate additional coverage metrics.

Function coverage is not a default Python gate. Combined statement-and-branch
coverage supplies meaningful evidence without custom tooling.

[%- endset %]

[% set TEST_LAYOUT -%]
The base Python scaffold generates only:

```text
tests/
└── test_package.py
```

`test_package.py` verifies that the installed `src` package imports
successfully. It protects the packaging contract and catches incorrect package
names, missing package data, broken imports and invalid build configuration.

Project capabilities may add `unit`, `integration`, `e2e`, `support`, or a
shared `conftest.py` only when those responsibilities exist. Do not generate
empty directories, mirror the entire `src` tree mechanically, or create
structures such as `tests/python`, `python_tests`, or `python_coverage`.

[%- endset %]

[% set TECHNOLOGY_TESTING_STANDARDS -%]
### Python testing

- Use pytest as the test runner.
- Keep pytest and Coverage.py configuration in `pyproject.toml`.
- Enable strict configuration and strict marker validation.
- Treat warnings as errors.
- Keep warning exclusions narrow and identify the exact warning.
- Register every custom marker.
- Treat unexpected `xfail` success as a failure.
- Do not use `skip` or `xfail` to hide a defect.
- Use plain test functions by default.
- Use test classes only for meaningful behavioral grouping without shared
  mutable state.
- Keep fixtures explicit, focused and at the narrowest useful scope.
- Avoid automatic fixtures unless they enforce a genuine suite-wide invariant.
- Do not hide the behavior under test inside fixture setup.
- Use parametrization when several cases exercise the same behavior and remain
  readable.
- Do not compress unrelated scenarios into one parametrized test.
- Assert the specific expected outcome.
- Assert error messages only when their text is part of the contract.
- Use `tmp_path` and other pytest-managed resources instead of shared paths.
- Do not depend on test execution order.
- Do not add pytest plugins without a concrete requirement.
- Keep live network access disabled in automated tests.

The scaffold includes one meaningful initial contract for the selected project
shape. It never generates DTO field tests, placeholder arithmetic tests,
`assert True`, or tests whose only purpose is producing coverage.

